      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Concrete wall crack repair is two separate jobs that people tend to blur together. One is sealing: stopping water, soil gas and insects from coming through a crack that is otherwise harmless. The other is structural: dealing with a wall that is moving because the soil around it or under it is pushing or settling. Injection kits, patch compounds and sealants handle the first job well. None of them handle the second. Spend ten minutes on diagnosis before you buy anything, because the right repair for a shrinkage crack is the wrong repair for a bowing wall.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="read-the-crack" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Read the Crack Before You Repair It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Poured concrete walls crack mostly because concrete shrinks as it cures. A 30 to 40 ft foundation wall shrinks measurably in its first year, and if there are no control joints, the wall relieves that stress by cracking, usually in the first few months after the pour. These shrinkage cracks are typically vertical or slightly diagonal, hairline to about 1/8 in wide, uniform in width from top to bottom, and often start at the corner of a basement window, a pipe penetration or a form tie hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shrinkage cracks are the bread and butter of poured concrete wall crack repair. They are not structural, they rarely grow after the first couple of years, and the main problem they cause is water. Groundwater finds the crack, wicks through it, and leaves a damp line or efflorescence after heavy rain. Sealing them, ideally with injection, is a legitimate, permanent fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other cracks need more attention. A crack that is wider at the top than the bottom, or vice versa, suggests part of the wall is settling or rotating. A diagonal crack from a corner that keeps growing can mean the footing is settling at that end. A horizontal crack in the middle third of the wall means soil pressure is pushing the wall inward. And any crack where you can feel a ridge, where one face sits proud of the other, means the wall has displaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            To know whether a crack is still active, monitor it. Pencil a line across the crack at a few points and mark the date. Measure the width at the same spots every few weeks with a crack card or a set of drill bits. Mark the ends, too, because a crack that grows in length is active even if it is not getting wider. Two to three months of readings through a seasonal change tells you far more than a single look.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">General Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical upper width of a shrinkage crack</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Width that warrants a professional look</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Monitoring through a season</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Vertical</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most common, least worrying</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Structural Warning Signs That Mean Stop and Call a Pro
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some cracks are not a DIY repair, and sealing them can actually make things worse by hiding what an engineer would need to see. The clearest warning is a horizontal crack, especially one running along the wall at roughly mid-height or a few feet below grade. That is where soil pressure, often from saturated or frozen clay, bends a wall inward. A horizontal crack with any visible bow, measured with a string line or a long level, is a structural issue.
          </p>

          <p>
            The next warning is displacement: one side of the crack sitting in front of the other, or a crack that steps across the wall in a way that suggests one section has dropped. Width matters too. Cracks over about 1/4 in are commonly treated as a threshold where a professional assessment is warranted, and any crack that you can document growing month over month belongs in the same category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look beyond the wall itself. Doors upstairs that suddenly stick, new drywall cracks at the corners of openings, gaps opening between the wall and the floor slab, or a wall that has separated from the sill plate are signs the house is moving with the foundation. In block foundations, stair-step cracks following the mortar joints are the classic sign of settlement or lateral pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            If any of these apply, have a structural engineer look before a contractor quotes. An engineer's report typically costs a few hundred dollars and tells you what the problem is without trying to sell a solution. Repairs for structural wall movement include wall anchors, carbon fiber straps, steel braces and, in severe cases, excavation and rebuilding. Our guide to <a href="/foundation-crack-repair-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">foundation crack repair cost</a> covers typical price ranges for each.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">STOP SIGNS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Horizontal, stair-step, bowing, over 1/4 in, or offset.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Any one of these means the wall is moving, not just shrinking. Document it with photos and measurements, and get a structural engineer's opinion before sealing anything.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="choose-method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Choosing a Concrete Wall Crack Repair Method
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a non-structural crack in a poured wall, there are three practical methods: low-pressure injection, V-groove patching, and surface sealing. Injection fills the crack through the full thickness of the wall, which is why it is the method foundation contractors use for leaking cracks. V-groove patching opens the crack face and packs it with a patching compound. Surface sealing caulks or coats the face of the crack only.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within injection, the choice is between polyurethane and epoxy. Polyurethane resin reacts with moisture, expands, and cures into a flexible foam or gel. It tolerates damp cracks and small seasonal movement and is the standard choice for leaks. Epoxy cures rigid and bonds the crack faces together, restoring strength. Manufacturers rate structural epoxies stronger than the concrete itself, but epoxy needs a dry, stable crack. Our <a href="/basement-crack-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack sealer guide</a> compares the two in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, most homeowners with a leaking vertical crack should inject polyurethane. Epoxy makes sense when an engineer wants a dry crack rebonded after the cause has been fixed. V-groove patching with hydraulic cement makes sense when water is actively flowing and needs to be stopped before anything else can happen. Surface caulk is fine for a dry crack you want to close off for radon, insects or appearance; see our article on <a href="/caulk-for-basement-wall-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">caulk for basement wall cracks</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            DIY injection kits bundle ports, a two-part epoxy surface paste and cartridges of polyurethane or epoxy resin, and typically cover around 8 to 10 ft of crack. They are a good value for one or two cracks. For broader context on kit types, read our <a href="/concrete-repair-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair kit guide</a>, and for crack repair across slabs, steps and walls, our main <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair guide</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Seals full depth</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Adds strength</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyurethane injection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leaking or damp, slightly active cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy injection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry, stable cracks needing rebonding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">V-groove with hydraulic cement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Actively flowing water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Outer 1 to 2 in only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">V-groove with epoxy or polymer patch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry cracks, cosmetic face repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Outer 1 to 2 in only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface caulk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry, stable cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="injection-steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Low-Pressure Injection With Surface Ports, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with prep. Use a wire wheel or a grinder with a cup wheel to remove paint, sealer, efflorescence and laitance from a strip about 1 to 2 in wide along the full length of the crack. The surface paste and ports need to bond to clean concrete. Do not grind the crack open or V-groove it for injection; you want the crack narrow at the surface so the paste can bridge it. Vacuum the dust and wear a silica-rated respirator and eye protection while grinding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, set the surface ports. Mark port locations every 6 to 12 in along the crack, closer on narrow cracks and farther apart on wider ones, with one near each end. Mix a small batch of the two-part epoxy paste, butter the base of each port, and press it centered over the crack. Some kits use a small nail or pin through the port to center it on the crack while the paste is applied. Do not let paste flow into the port opening or into the crack beneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then cap the crack. Mix more paste and spread it in a band about 1 in wide and 1/8 to 1/4 in thick over the crack between ports and around each port base, feathering the edges. This cap holds the resin in the wall during injection. Let it cure according to the label, often an hour or more, longer in a cold basement. If the crack runs through the full wall and you can reach the outside, some contractors also check that the exterior is not wide open, since resin can escape there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inject from the bottom up. Load the resin cartridge into the dispensing gun, attach it to the lowest port, and pump slowly and steadily. Low pressure and patience matter: the resin needs time to move through the wall. When resin appears at the next port up, stop, cap or plug the port you are on, and move to the next one. Continue to the top. With polyurethane, you may see foam push out of hairline gaps in the paste; dab them closed with paste or wait for the foam to cure. With epoxy, many contractors return after 10 to 20 minutes and top up ports where resin has soaked in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, finish. Let the resin cure per the label, often 24 hours for epoxy and less for polyurethane. Then knock the ports off with a hammer or chisel and grind the paste flush if the wall will be finished or painted. Many people leave the paste on an unfinished basement wall, since grinding it off creates dust and there is no functional benefit. Test the repair with the next heavy rain, or by soaking the soil outside the crack with a hose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="v-groove" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. V-Groove Patching: When Injection Is Not the Right Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            V-groove patching opens the face of the crack into a groove and packs it with a repair material. It was the standard approach before injection kits were common, and it still has a place. The obvious case is a crack with water flowing out of it: injection paste will not cure on a wall with water running down it, but hydraulic cement will set in minutes in a wet groove.
          </p>

          <p>
            To do it, cut along the crack with a grinder and diamond blade, or a cold chisel and hammer, to make a groove about 1/2 to 3/4 in wide and similarly deep. Undercut the edges slightly if you can, so the groove is wider at the bottom than the top and the patch is locked in. Clean out all dust and loose material, then wet the groove for cementitious products. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-hydraulic-cement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use hydraulic cement</a> covers the fast-set technique, including starting at the top and working down on a leaking crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limitation is depth. A V-groove patch fills the outer inch or so of an 8 to 10 in wall. Water still enters the crack from the outside and travels through the wall behind the patch, where it can find a path around the edges or come out at the next weak spot. And a rigid patch in a crack that moves seasonally tends to debond along its edges. Owners commonly report V-groove repairs that worked for a year or two and then began to weep at the patch line.
          </p>

          <p>
            So use V-groove patching as either an emergency stop or a cosmetic face repair. For a flowing leak, plug it with hydraulic cement, let the wall dry, then inject through ports set alongside the patch for a full-depth seal. For a dry crack that only needs a neat surface before finishing the basement, an epoxy or polymer-modified patching compound in a shallow groove works well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="block-walls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Concrete Block Walls Need a Different Approach
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything above about injection assumes a solid poured concrete wall. Concrete block, also called CMU, behaves differently. A block wall is a grid of hollow units held together by mortar joints, and cracks usually follow the mortar in a stair-step pattern or run straight through a vertical column of joints. Behind the face of the block are open cores that may be partly filled with water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Injection rarely works in block, because resin pumped into a crack in the face shell runs into the hollow core instead of sealing the crack. You would need to fill the core first, which is not practical. Block wall water problems are usually handled with drainage: exterior grading and gutters, interior perimeter drains that relieve water at the base of the wall, and masonry coatings on the interior face. See our guide to <a href="/hydrostatic-pressure-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hydrostatic pressure in a basement</a> for how drainage relieves the pressure that drives water through block.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cracked mortar joints in a stable wall, repointing is the repair: rake out the damaged mortar to about 3/4 in, then pack in new mortar of a compatible type. Small cracks in the face of a block can be patched with a polymer-modified masonry patch or sealed with a masonry sealant. For damp but uncracked block, a masonry waterproofing coating can reduce seepage; our <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement wall sealer</a> rankings cover the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Block walls are also more prone to structural movement than poured walls, because the mortar joints are weak in tension. A stair-step crack that grows, a horizontal crack along a mortar joint, or a block wall that bows inward are all reasons to stop and call an engineer. Block walls under lateral pressure are commonly reinforced with wall anchors, steel beams or carbon fiber straps, none of which are DIY repairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. After the Repair: Testing, Finishing and Preventing New Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            A repaired crack is only proven after it has seen real water. Wait for a heavy rain, or run a hose on the soil outside the crack for 20 to 30 minutes, and check the inside face. With injection, a small weep at one spot usually means the resin did not fully travel there, and that section can be reinjected through a new port. With a V-groove patch, a weep along the patch edge means the patch has debonded and needs either redoing or a follow-up injection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before finishing the basement, give the repair a full wet season. Framing and drywall over a crack that has not yet been tested invites hidden mold if it leaks. If you are insulating the wall, use materials that tolerate occasional moisture, such as rigid foam board against the concrete, rather than fiberglass batts directly against it. Leave a way to inspect the repaired section if you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevention is mostly about water outside the wall. Gutters that overflow and downspouts that dump next to the foundation saturate the soil right where cracks are. Extend downspouts at least 4 to 6 ft out, keep the soil sloped away from the house, and avoid planting beds that hold water against the wall. Saturated soil is heavier and pushes harder, which is how a harmless shrinkage crack becomes a leak and, over time, how lateral pressure builds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then keep watching. Recheck your pencil marks a few times a year, especially in late winter and early spring when frost and wet soil put the most pressure on walls. A sealed shrinkage crack should stay dry and unchanged. If it widens or a new crack appears beside it, the wall is telling you something injection cannot fix. For leaks rising through the floor rather than the walls, see our <a href="/basement-floor-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement floor crack repair</a> guide, and for the full set of interior and exterior leak fixes, our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack repair</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">General Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-30 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hose test on soil outside</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4-6 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Downspout extension from wall</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 season</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wait before finishing the wall</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-4x/yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Recheck crack marks</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">V-grooving before injecting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Injection relies on a narrow crack at the surface so the epoxy paste can bridge it and hold resin in the wall. Grinding a V-groove first creates a wide channel the paste cannot cap cleanly, and resin leaks out instead of traveling through the wall. Clean the surface for injection; do not open the crack.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Injecting epoxy into a wet, active crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy needs a dry, stable crack to bond and fill. Water in the crack weakens the bond and blocks the resin, and rigid cured epoxy in a crack that still moves just shifts the crack beside it. For leaking or seasonally active cracks, polyurethane is the better resin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pumping too fast or too hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Low-pressure injection means slow and steady. Pumping hard can blow the surface paste or a port off the wall, and resin that moves too quickly can skip voids. Inject from the lowest port, wait for resin to show at the next, then move up, topping up where the resin soaks in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a horizontal or bowing crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A horizontal crack, a bow, or one side of the crack offset from the other means soil pressure or settlement is moving the wall. Injection seals the crack but does nothing about the force. It also hides evidence an engineer needs. Measure, photograph and get a structural assessment first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Finishing the wall before testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Framing and drywall over a freshly repaired crack that has never seen a heavy storm is a gamble. If the repair weeps, the moisture stays hidden behind the finish and feeds mold. Let the repair go through a full wet season, or hose-test it, before covering the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I repair a poured concrete wall crack myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for non-structural cracks. Vertical or slightly diagonal shrinkage cracks under about 1/8 in that are not growing are good DIY candidates, and a low-pressure injection kit can seal them through the full wall thickness. Horizontal cracks, bowing walls, displacement, or cracks over about 1/4 in should be assessed by a structural engineer before any repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyurethane or epoxy injection better for foundation wall cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polyurethane is usually better for leaking cracks, because it stays flexible, tolerates damp conditions and handles small seasonal movement. Epoxy is better for dry, stable cracks that need structural rebonding, because it cures rigid and bonds the crack faces together. Many contractors use polyurethane for water problems and reserve epoxy for engineer-specified structural repairs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far apart should injection ports be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kits and contractors commonly space surface ports about 6 to 12 in apart along the crack, with closer spacing on very narrow cracks where resin travels more slowly and wider spacing on larger cracks. Put a port near each end of the crack. During injection, resin should appear at the next port before you move up, which confirms the spacing worked.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does hydraulic cement fix a concrete wall crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It stops water fast, but it is rarely a permanent crack repair on its own. Hydraulic cement fills only the outer inch or two of the crack and cures rigid, so in a crack that moves seasonally it tends to debond along its edges. Use it to stop an active leak, then inject the crack for a full-depth seal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does crack injection take to cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the resin and the temperature. The epoxy surface paste often needs about an hour or more before you can inject. Polyurethane resins typically react and set within minutes to hours. Injection epoxies commonly need around 24 hours before ports can be removed, longer in a cold basement. Always follow the specific kit's label times.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do block walls crack differently from poured walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Block walls are built from hollow units joined by mortar, which is weak in tension, so cracks follow the mortar joints in stair-step or straight vertical lines. Poured walls are monolithic and crack mostly from shrinkage. That is why injection works well in poured walls but poorly in block, where resin runs into the hollow cores instead of sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a horizontal crack in a basement wall mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A horizontal crack in a basement wall usually means soil pressure from outside is pushing the wall inward, often from saturated or frozen soil. It is one of the more serious foundation warning signs, especially with any bow in the wall. Do not seal it and move on. Have a structural engineer assess it and recommend bracing or anchoring if needed.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

