        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most cracks in poured concrete walls are shrinkage cracks, and low-pressure injection seals them permanently. Polyurethane for leaks, epoxy for dry cracks that need strength, surface ports every 6 to 12 in, bottom up, slow and steady. Hydraulic cement in a V-groove is the emergency stop, not the finished repair.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The skill that matters most is knowing when not to repair. Horizontal cracks, stair-step cracks in block, bowing, offset and cracks wider than 1/4 in are signs the wall is moving. Measure them, photograph them, and let an engineer tell you what is pushing before anyone seals the evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

